"""patch_prereg_addendum10a_1030.py (2026-10-30): appends Addendum 10a (what the premium builds and diagnostics found, and the reading rule fixed before any premium is read)
to results/PREREG_g7_proven_rerun_2026-10-29.md. The registration must be exactly the version after Addendum 10 (sha256 3e7bc7b5...) and the placeholder must occur
exactly once, or nothing is changed. No registered prediction is edited: the text before PRED-TABLE-END is compared before and after.
Usage: python3 scripts/audit/patch_prereg_addendum10a_1030.py [results/PREREG_g7_proven_rerun_2026-10-29.md]"""
import hashlib, sys
def sha(s): return hashlib.sha256((s if s.endswith("\n") else s + "\n").encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()
p = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else "results/PREREG_g7_proven_rerun_2026-10-29.md"
T = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
assert sha(T) == "3e7bc7b5abb441009a5ce7ee5306aff0ec624faa92980a5aaf0974dfe455d0bb", "the registration is not the version after Addendum 10; nothing changed"
old = "(further dated addenda go here: the remaining procedures)"
assert T.count(old) == 1, "placeholder not found exactly once; nothing changed"
new = """### Addendum 10a (2026-10-30, after the builds and the diagnostics, before any premium was read): what was found and the reading rule

**The builds, as run.** ARM 0 returned 5,892, 6,342 and 7,818 rows for the three calls (20,052 in all) and ARM 1 5,891, 6,337 and 7,817 (20,045), seven fewer, as the tripwire expected; no call timed out. The stored table has 16,993 rows. QUERY 1 of the committed file returned: arm0_in_both 19,932, only_in_arm0 120, only_in_stored 0, common_rows_that_differ 1, arm0_rows_with_exclude_row 7, in_arm0_not_arm1 7. QUERY 1 counts matched PAIRS, so it double-counts as soon as a key repeats; this is a flaw in its design, found because arm0_in_both exceeded the number of stored rows.

**Diagnostics added after seeing those counts (descriptive only; no premium was read; they are not in the committed files).** (D1) The stored table has 16,993 distinct (event_id, ticker) keys and no repeated rows; ARM 0 has 17,112 distinct keys and 2,940 repeated rows; ARM 1 has 17,106 keys and 2,939 repeated rows. The cause: 4,968 of the 4,971 rows of reactions_view_only are now also in event_market_reactions_corrected, although by design the view-only table holds the events that are not in the corrected one, and the function takes the UNION ALL of the two, so such an event is emitted twice. No source repeats a key inside itself, no entity and ticker pair is repeated in securities, and 46 events have two realized-volatility rows (the diagnostics do not separate any effect of those from the source overlap). (D3) The repeated rows agree to within 1e-8 in scaled in 2,939 of the 2,940 key groups, to within 1e-6 in ratio in all of them, and in month and bucket in all of them; ONE key group conflicts, its two scaled values differing by 0.0914. (D4) The 119 keys that are new relative to the stored table are 2 in 2019, 1 in 2026-06, 93 in 2026-08 and 23 in 2026-09; the months from 2026-08 have no row in mm_matched (its last month is 2026-07-01), so those 116 keys drop out of any premium whatever is done, and only 3 new keys can enter. (D2) The repeats are spread over every year (for example 86 of 481 rows in 2004, about 8 to 12 percent of rows in each of 2005 to 2018, about 19 to 24 percent in each of 2019 to 2025).

**The reading rule, fixed now.** The build files stay exactly as committed: ARM 0 is the original procedure on today's inputs, repeats included. The premium is READ on one row per event and ticker, the mean of the repeated rows' scaled and ratio with their month and bucket (which agree), for the stored table, ARM 0 and ARM 1; the raw ARM 0 and ARM 1, repeats included, are reported beside them as the literal output of the original procedure, with the caution that their naive SEs are understated by the double counting. The new tables do not record which source a row came from, so a source priority cannot be applied; the mean is neutral, identical to either row for 2,939 of 2,940 key groups, and for the one conflicting group its effect on any mean is bounded by 0.0914 divided by about 17,000, that is 0.000005. The amended query is scripts/sql/g7_premium_compare_amended_2026-10-30.sql and replaces QUERY 2 of the committed file; the registered p33 and p34 are read on ARM 1 de-duplicated, and the raw values are reported beside them. The scoring conventions of Addendum 10 (categories A to D, p35, p36) are unchanged.

**Tripwires, restated.** (1) The stored arm of the amended query must reproduce the pooled 2004-2018 estimate +0.0550 on 9,412 rows and the six era and cohort values of the ledger. (2) The de-duplicated ARM 0 may differ from the stored table by at most the 3 new keys that have a baseline (2 in 2019, 1 in 2026-06); more than that stops the reading and is attributed first. (3) The de-duplicated ARM 1 may differ from the de-duplicated ARM 0 by at most the 7 events with an exclude row.

**Stated in advance.** (a) reactions_view_only is now almost entirely redundant with event_market_reactions_corrected (4,968 of 4,971 rows), so any query that unions the two double-counts those events; the other consumers of the two tables are not known and are listed for checking, and nothing is changed here. (b) The one conflicting key group is not identified here and is not corrected. (c) A rebuild is therefore, after de-duplication, the stored table plus 3 keys; it tests that nothing drifted, not that the premium is new. Nothing here re-scores or edits a prediction.

(further dated addenda go here: the remaining procedures)"""
head_before = T[:T.index("PRED-TABLE-END")]
R = T.replace(old, new)
assert R[:R.index("PRED-TABLE-END")] == head_before, "a registered prediction would change; nothing written"
open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(R)
R2 = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read(); print("ok   Addendum 10a appended"); print("PREREG sha256", sha(R2), "|", R2.count("\n"), "lines")
